[Event "Rook endgames: behind the passed pawn, two pawns, two rooks behind them"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/nwNSjIDY"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Source "Lichess puzzle 4nPM1, rated 1576, https://lichess.org/c6QjM1fj/black#126"]
[FEN "3R4/4KP2/8/8/8/8/1p1k4/5r2 b - - 5 63"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/3R4/4KP2/8/8/8/8/1p1k4/5r2_b_-_-_5_63"]

{ Rook endings turn up in more games than any other ending. First idea: behind the passed pawn, on its column and on the side it came from, where a rook keeps the pawn in sight all the way to its last square. [%cal Rf1f7] }
63... Ke2 { His king steps away to e2 and leaves his b pawn alone: a mistake, it had to step next to the pawn. Now get your rook behind that pawn: rook to b8. [%cal Yd8b8] } 64. Rb8 { Your rook stands behind his b pawn now, watching it all the way down. His rook on f1 does the same to your pawn, so f8 is no place for a queen yet. [%cal Gb8b1,Rf1f8] } (64. f8=Q { Queening at once is what everybody wants, but his rook on f1 stood behind your pawn, and it sees f8. [%cal Rf1f8] } 64... Rxf8 65. Rxf8 b1=Q { Your queen lasted one move, and now his pawn becomes a queen with nothing behind it. A queen against your rook: you lose. [%csl Rb1] }) (64. Rd2+ { A check from d2 puts your rook right next to his king. [%csl Rd2] } 64... Kxd2 { His king takes your rook, and his pawn on b2 is one step from a queen. You lose. }) (64. Rd1 { Rook to d1 guards his queening square from the side, but his king on e2 stands right next to your rook. [%cal Rd1b1] } 64... Kxd1 { His king takes your rook. A guard from the side can be reached; a rook far behind the pawn, on the b column, could not. You lose. }) (64. Kd7 { Your king sets off after his b pawn, but it is far too slow, and your f pawn is left on its own. } 64... Rxf7+ 65. Ke6 Rb7 66. Rd5 b1=Q { His rook took your pawn with check and stepped behind his own b pawn, and that pawn is a queen now. You lose. [%cal Rb7b1] }) (64. Kd6 { King to d6 also heads for his b pawn, and leaves your rook where it was. } 64... b1=Q { His pawn becomes a queen while your king is still far away. He has a queen and a rook against your rook and pawn: you lose. [%csl Rb1] }) 64... b1=Q { He queens on b1 anyway, and your rook is looking straight at her. } 65. Rxb1 { The new queen lasts one move: your rook was waiting behind the pawn. } (65. f8=Q { Making your own queen first lets his queen move first, with check. } 65... Qe4+ 66. Kd7 Rxf8 67. Rxf8 { His queen checked first, and then his rook took your new queen. His queen against your rook: you lose. [%csl Rf8] }) 65... Rxb1 { His rook takes back on b1, and it has left the f column. Nothing stands behind your pawn now. } 66. f8=Q { A new queen on f8, and nothing can take her. Your rook behind his pawn took his queen and dragged his rook off your column: that is the rook behind the passed pawn. [%csl Gf8] } 1-0


[Event "Rook endgames: behind the passed pawn, his rook watches your pawn"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/Mvl50LtL"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Source "Lichess puzzle fQljq, rated 1409, https://lichess.org/yobAZCSF/black#102"]
[FEN "8/8/2P5/3K1pp1/1R3k2/8/8/2r5 b - - 1 51"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/2P5/3K1pp1/1R3k2/8/8/2r5_b_-_-_1_51"]

{ You have White, a pawn down. }
51... Kg3 { His king walks the wrong way, to g3: a mistake. His rook on c1 sits behind your pawn and keeps it in sight all the way to its last square. What now? [%csl Rc1] } 52. Rc4 { Rook to c4 steps between his rook and your pawn: now your rook is behind your pawn. His rook sees only yours, and your king on d5 guards it. [%cal Gc4c8] } (52. c7 { Pushing at once is natural, but his rook on c1 stands behind your pawn and sees c7. } 52... Rxc7 { His rook takes your pawn from behind. His rook and two pawns against your rook: you lose. [%csl Rc7] }) (52. Rb6 { Rook to b6 guards your pawn from the side, but only where it stands: it cannot go forward with it. } 52... f4 53. c7 Rxc7 { When your pawn stepped to c7, your rook on b6 no longer guarded it, and his rook took it. You lose. [%csl Rc7] }) (52. Rb3+ { A check from b3 moves his king, but your rook still stands beside your pawn's column, not behind it. } 52... Kf2 53. Rb2+ Kg3 54. Rb3+ { Your checks only move his king back and forth, and his rook on c1 still watches your pawn: only a draw. }) (52. Ra4 { Rook to a4 stays on the fourth row and leaves the c column to his rook. } 52... f4 53. Rc4 { Now your rook is behind your pawn, but a move late: his f pawn has already run to f4. Only a draw. [%csl Rf4] }) (52. Rd4 { Rook to d4 shields your king from checks on the d column, but your pawn stays under his rook's eye. } 52... f4 { His f pawn runs on while his rook on c1 still watches your pawn: only a draw. }) (52. Rb5 { Rook to b5 guards the fifth row, but leaves the c column to his rook. } 52... f4 53. Rc5 { Your rook gets behind your pawn at last, but his f pawn used the time to reach f4: only a draw. }) (52. Kd6 { King to d6 stays next to your pawn, but his rook still stands behind it. [%cal Rc1c6] } 52... f4 53. c7 f3 54. c8=Q Rxc8 { Your pawn made a queen, and his rook took her at once from c1: it watched your pawn all the way. You lose. [%csl Rc8] }) (52. Ke5 { King to e5 goes after his pawns, but leaves your pawn to his rook. } 52... f4 53. c7 Rxc7 { He saved his f pawn from your king first, and when your pawn stepped to c7 his rook took it from behind. You lose. }) (52. Kd4 { King to d4 steps away from your pawn, and nothing guards the pawn any more. } 52... Rxc6 { His rook takes your pawn from behind, all the way up the c column: his rook and two pawns against your rook, you lose. [%csl Rc6] }) 52... Rd1+ { His rook leaves the c column and checks your king from d1. } 53. Ke5 { King to e5 steps out of the check toward his pawns, and stays off the c column, where it would block your rook. } (53. Ke6 { King to e6 also wins: it attacks his f5 pawn just as well, and stays off the c column. } 53... Rd8 { His rook goes back to d8 to stop your pawn, and only taking f5 at once still wins. }) (53. Kc5 { King to c5 steps onto the c column, between your rook and your pawn. } 53... Rd8 54. c7 Rc8 { His rook stands on c8, in front of your pawn, and your king blocks your own rook: only a draw. [%csl Rc5] }) 53... Ra1 { His rook swings to a1, ready to check from the side. } 54. c7 { Push to c7: your pawn walks, and your rook behind it guards every step. [%cal Gc4c7] } (54. Kxf5 { Taking his f5 pawn at once also wins: one pawn fewer for him, and your rook still stands behind your pawn. } 54... Ra5+ { His rook checks along the fifth row, and only king to e4 still wins: a harder road than pushing your pawn. }) (54. Rc3+ { A check from c3 also wins: your rook stays on the c column, behind your pawn. [%cal Gc3c6] } 54... Kg4 { His king steps next to both his pawns, but your pawn can still walk on to c7: a win. }) (54. Kd6 { King to d6 goes to help your pawn, but leaves his pawns free. } 54... f4 { His f pawn runs at once, and your king on d6 is no longer next to it: only a draw. }) 54... Ra5+ { His rook checks you along the fifth row. } 55. Ke6 { King to e6 dodges the check and stays next to his f5 pawn. } (55. Kd4 { King to d4 also wins: out of the check, while your rook still keeps your pawn in sight. } 55... Ra8 { Back to a8 he goes, to stop your pawn: now only queening at once still wins, and his rook must give itself up for the new queen. }) (55. Kf6 { King to f6 also wins: it goes after his pawns, and your rook behind your pawn keeps working. } 55... Ra8 { His rook comes back to a8, and your king next to his f5 pawn can take it: still a win. }) (55. Kd6 { King to d6 stays with your pawn and walks away from his. } 55... Ra8 { His rook comes back to a8 to stop your pawn, and your king is far from his pawns now: only a draw. }) 55... Ra8 { His rook goes back to a8, ready to stop your pawn from queening. What now? } 56. Kxf5 { King takes f5 first. Queening now would only trade your queen for his rook, and then his two pawns with his king could hold your rook. [%csl Gf5] } (56. Rc3+ { Rook to c3, with check, also keeps the win. } 56... Kf4 { His king stands on f4, next to both his pawns, and only another check, from c4, keeps the win. }) (56. c8=Q { The new queen at once: his rook simply gives itself up for her. } 56... Rxc8 57. Rxc8 f4 { Your rook took back, but now his two pawns and his king hold your lone rook: only a draw. [%csl Rf4] }) (56. Kd7 { King to d7 goes to help your pawn, but his pawns are left alone. } 56... f4 { His f pawn runs on while your king is busy with your own pawn: only a draw. }) 56... Rc8 { His rook steps in front of your pawn, onto c8. } 57. Kxg5 { King takes g5 as well, and his pawns are gone: your rook and pawn against his rook, with your rook still behind the pawn. [%tail] [%cal Gc4c7] [%csl Gg5] } (57. Ke6 { King to e6 heads for your pawn and lets his g pawn live. } 57... Kh3 58. Rc3+ Kg4 { His king has joined his g pawn, and yours is far away on e6: only a draw. }) 57... Rg8+ 58. Kf5 Rc8 59. Ke6 Re8+ 60. Kd7 Ra8 61. c8=Q Rxc8 62. Rxc8 { Your rook takes back, and his king stands alone against your rook: a win. Your rook was behind your pawn all the way. His king on e3, instead of g3, would have held the draw. } 1-0


[Event "Rook endgames: behind the passed pawn, the stuck pawn"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/2ffkRzck"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Source "Reti - Rubinstein, Teplitz-Schoenau 1922 (pgnmentor)"]
[FEN "2r5/6P1/3k4/8/7P/5K2/8/4R3 b - - 2 66"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2r5/6P1/3k4/8/7P/5K2/8/4R3_b_-_-_2_66"]

{ You have White, two pawns up. }
66... Rg8 { His rook goes to g8 and attacks your g7 pawn, one step from a queen. What now? } 67. Rg1 { Rook to g1 gets behind your g pawn: it guards g7 along the whole column, so his rook cannot take it without being taken. [%cal Gg1g7] } (67. h5 { Pushing the h pawn first leaves g7 with no guard. } 67... Rxg7 68. h6 Rh7 { His rook took g7 and now sits in front of your h pawn: a rook and pawn against his rook, only a draw. [%csl Rh7] }) (67. Rd1+ { A check first, but it gives his rook time to take g7. } 67... Ke5 68. h5 Rxg7 { His rook took your g pawn while your rook was busy checking: only a draw. }) (67. Re7 { Rook to e7 guards g7 from the side, but his king takes it. [%cal Re7g7] } 67... Kxe7 { His king took your rook. Your two pawns are no match for his rook: you lose. }) (67. Kg4 { King to g4 walks toward your pawns, but leaves g7 to his rook, with check. } 67... Rxg7+ { His rook took your g pawn with check, and your h pawn alone only draws. }) 67... Ke6 { His king heads for f7, to attack your g7 pawn a second time. [%cal Re6f7] } 68. h5 { Your h pawn runs to help: from h6 it can guard g7 too, and it gets there just in time. [%cal Gh5h6,Bh6g7] } (68. Kg4 { King to g4 steps onto the g column, in line with your own rook. } 68... Rxg7+ 69. Kf3 Rxg1 { His rook took g7 with check, and when your king stepped off the column it took your rook as well. You lose. [%cal Rg7g1] }) (68. Kf4 { King to f4 walks toward his king, but your pawns stay where they were. } 68... Kf6 69. h5 Rxg7 70. Rxg7 Kxg7 { His king came to f6 to attack g7 twice, the rooks came off, and his king is close enough to stop your h pawn: only a draw. }) (68. Re1+ { A check from e1, but your rook leaves the g column, and g7 loses its guard. } 68... Kf5 69. h5 Rxg7 { His rook took g7 the moment your rook stepped away: this draws. }) 68... Kf7 { His king reaches f7: two attackers on your g7 pawn now. } 69. h6 { Pawn to h6 guards g7 a second time: two guards against his two attackers, so your g pawn stays. [%tail] [%cal Gh6g7,Gg1g7] } (69. Kg4 { King to g4 puts it on the g column, in line with your rook. } 69... Rxg7+ 70. Kf3 Rxg1 { His rook took g7 with check and then your rook as well, both on the same column. You lose. [%cal Rg7g1] }) (69. Kf4 { King to f4 lets his rook take g7 before your h pawn can guard it. } 69... Rxg7 70. Rxg7+ Kxg7 { The rooks came off on g7, and his king stands right next to your h pawn's road: only a draw. }) 69... Ra8 70. h7 { Not a queen on g8: it falls to his rook, and his king catches the h pawn. } 70... Ra3+ 71. Ke2 Ra2+ 72. Kd3 Ra3+ 73. Kc2 Ra2+ 74. Kb3 Rh2 75. h8=Q Rxh8 76. gxh8=Q { A safe queen on h8. The great endgame master Rubinstein could not stop your rook behind the pawn. } 1-0


[Event "Rook endgames: behind the passed pawn, his pawn runs"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/k0kfh5OI"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]
[Source "Pillsbury - Lasker, St Petersburg 1895/96 (pgnmentor)"]
[FEN "8/8/2k5/1R6/P1P1p3/1K6/8/r7 b - - 3 47"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/2k5/1R6/P1P1p3/1K6/8/r7_b_-_-_3_47"]

{ You have White, a pawn up: hold the draw. }
47... e3 { His pawn runs to e3, two steps from a queen, and his rook on a1 already guards the first row. Hold the draw: what now? } 48. Re5 { Rook to e5 gets behind his pawn, on its column and on the side it came from: it keeps the pawn in sight all the way to e1. [%cal Ge5e1] } (48. Rc5+ { A check from c5, right next to his king. } 48... Kxc5 { His king takes your rook. His rook and pawn against your two pawns: you lose. }) (48. Rb4 { Rook to b4 guards your pawns from the side, but it does nothing about his pawn. } 48... Rb1+ 49. Kc3 Rxb4 50. Kxb4 e2 { His rook took yours and your king took back, but from b4 your king cannot catch his pawn, one step from a queen: you lose. }) (48. Ra5 { Rook to a5 stands in front of your own a pawn, far from his pawn. } 48... Rb1+ 49. Ka2 e2 50. Kxb1 e1=Q+ { Your king took his rook, but his pawn became a queen with check: a queen against your rook, you lose. }) (48. Kc3 { King to c3 walks toward his pawn, but the pawn is faster. } 48... e2 49. Rb1 Rxb1 { His rook took yours, and nothing can stop his pawn now: you lose. }) (48. Kc2 { King to c2 heads for his queening square, one step too slow. } 48... e2 49. Rb1 Rxb1 50. Kxb1 e1=Q+ { The rooks are gone, and his pawn became a queen: a queen against two pawns, you lose. }) (48. Rb6+ { A check from b6, but his king takes it. } 48... Kxb6 { His king takes your rook, and his pawn is free to run: you lose. }) (48. Rd5 { Rook to d5 stays on the fifth row, but one column away from his pawn. } 48... e2 49. Re5 Rb1+ 50. Ka2 e1=Q { Your rook reached his pawn's column one move late: his check came first, and now his new queen is guarded by his rook. You lose. [%cal Rb1e1] }) (48. c5 { Pushing your own pawn to c5 ignores his: his pawn runs on. } 48... e2 { His pawn steps to e2, one step from a queen, and his rook already guards the queening square: you lose. }) (48. a5 { Pushing your a pawn starts a race you cannot win: his pawn is much closer to a queen. } 48... e2 { His pawn is on e2 already, and nothing of yours stands behind it: you lose. }) (48. Kb4 { King to b4 walks away from his pawn. } 48... e2 49. Rc5+ Kd6 50. Rd5+ Ke6 { Your checks only walked his king over to e6, and his pawn on e2 is one step from a queen: you lose. }) (48. Kb2 { King to b2 attacks his rook, but his pawn does not wait. } 48... e2 49. Kxa1 e1=Q+ { Your king won his rook, and his pawn queened with check: his queen against your rook, you lose. }) 48... Rb1+ { His rook checks your king from b1. } 49. Kc2 { King to c2 steps out of the check and attacks his rook. Your rook stays behind his pawn. [%tail] [%cal Ge5e3] } (49. Kc3 { King to c3 also holds: it steps toward his pawn, and your rook behind it still keeps it in sight. } 49... Kd6 { His king attacks your rook on e5, but your rook can stay behind his pawn: still a draw. }) (49. Ka2 { King to a2 also holds: it attacks his rook on b1, and your rook still watches his pawn from behind. } 49... Re1 { His rook slides to e1 to guard his pawn, and you hold just the same: a draw. }) (49. Ka3 { King to a3 also holds: out of the check, with your rook still behind his pawn. } 49... Kd6 { His king steps to d6 and hits your rook, which can still stay behind his pawn on the e column: a draw. }) 49... Re1 { His rook slides along to e1 to guard his pawn: taking it would cost your rook. } 50. a5 Re2+ 51. Kb1 Re1+ 52. Kc2 Re2+ 53. Kb1 Re1+ 54. Kc2 { The same position for the third time: a draw. Your rook behind his pawn kept it in sight, and his rook had to guard it. } 1-0


[Event "Rook endgames: cut off, two pawns and a wall"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/9UrVwI97"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Source "Kupchik - Capablanca, New York 1915 (pgnmentor)"]
[FEN "2r5/R7/6k1/8/8/6p1/4K1p1/8 w - - 0 48"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2r5/R7/6k1/8/8/6p1/4K1p1/8_w_-_-_0_48"]

{ You have Black and play to win. Cut off: his king is cut off when your rook guards a whole column between his king and your pawn, so he can never cross to help. }
48. Ra1 { His rook goes back to the first row to stop your g pawn. Capablanca, the Cuban who later became world champion, found a quiet move here: rook to f8. [%cal Yc8f8] } 48... Rf8 { Now your rook guards the whole f column, and his king on e2 can never cross it to help. That is the cut-off. [%cal Gf8f1] } (48... Rc3 { Rook to c3 wins too, more slowly: it guards your g3 pawn along the row, but nothing guards your front pawn. } 49. Rg1 Kf5 50. Rxg2 Kf4 { Still a win, but slower: he ate one pawn, and your king and rook now escort the last one. [%csl Gg3] }) (48... Ra8 { Rook to a8 wins too, more slowly: it attacks his rook on a1. } 49. Rg1 Kg5 50. Kf3 Kh4 51. Kxg2 Ra2+ { Still a win, only slower: his king ate the front pawn, but your rook checks it, and your king guards the last pawn. [%csl Gg3] }) (48... g1=Q { A queen at once? His rook on a1 is waiting for her. } 49. Rxg1 Rc2+ 50. Kd3 Ra2 51. Rxg3+ { A draw: both your pawns are gone, and it is rook against rook. Pushing first threw the win away. [%csl Rg3] }) (48... Re8+ { A check pushes his king forward, toward your pawns. } 49. Kf3 Rf8+ 50. Kxg2 { A draw: his king crossed the f column and ate your front pawn, and the pawn that is left is not enough to win. [%csl Rg2] }) (48... Rc2+ { A check from c2 drives his king forward, onto the f column. } 49. Kf3 Rf2+ 50. Kxg3 { A draw: his king walked through to g3 and ate a pawn, and it now stands right beside the other one. [%csl Rg3] }) (48... Kf5 { Your king hurries toward its pawns, but his king is faster. } 49. Kf3 Rc3+ 50. Kxg2 { A draw: with no wall on the f column, his king stepped across to f3 and ate your front pawn. [%csl Rg2] }) (48... Kg5 { Your king heads for its pawns. } 49. Kf3 Rc3+ 50. Kxg2 { A draw: nothing shut the f column, so his king got to f3 first, and your front pawn fell. [%csl Rf3] }) (48... Kh5 { Your king runs along the h column to guard its pawns. } 49. Kf3 Rc3+ 50. Kxg2 { A draw: no rook stood on the f column, so his king walked in and took your front pawn. [%csl Rg2] }) 49. Rg1 { His rook stands in front of your pawns, on g1. Now check him from f2: your rook stays on the f column, so the wall stands. [%cal Yf8f2] } 49... Rf2+ { Check from f2, where your g3 pawn guards your rook: his king has to move, and it still cannot cross. Now watch your king walk to its pawns. [%tail] [%cal Gf2f8] } (49... Kg5 { King to g5 wins too, more slowly: it walks toward your pawns at once. } 50. Rxg2 Kg4 51. Rg1 Rf2+ { Still a win, a little slower: he took one pawn, but your king and rook escort the other, and the f column stays shut. [%cal Gf2f8] }) (49... Kh5 { King to h5 wins too, by a longer road. } 50. Rxg2 Kh4 51. Rg1 Rf2+ { Also a win, just slower: one pawn is lost, yet your king on h4 guards the other and your rook still holds the f column. [%csl Gg3] }) (49... Kf5 { Your king steps onto the f column, right in front of your own rook. } 50. Kf3 Ke5+ 51. Kxg2 { A draw: your king blocked your own wall for one move, his king stepped through, and now it has eaten a pawn. [%csl Rg2] }) (49... Re8+ { A check from e8, and your rook leaves the f column. } 50. Kf3 Rf8+ 51. Kg4 { A draw: his king slipped across the f column to g4, and it now attacks your pawn on g3. [%csl Rg4] }) (49... Kf6 { Your king steps to f6, onto your own wall. } 50. Rxg2 Re8+ 51. Kf3 Kf5 52. Rxg3 { A draw: his rook ate both your pawns, and rook against rook is level. [%csl Rg3] }) 50. Ke3 Kf5 51. Kd3 Kg4 52. Ke4 Kh3 53. Ra1 Rf1 54. Ra6 g1=Q { A new queen on g1! His king wandered around but never once crossed the f column: that is the cut-off. [%csl Gg1][%cal Gf1f8] } 0-1


[Event "Rook endgames: cut off, his king is coming"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/MtnNyz1v"]
[Role "exercise"]
