[IntendedResult "win"]
[Source "Richter - Alekhine, Munich 1941 (pgnmentor)"]
[FEN "8/8/4kp2/4r3/8/R7/8/1K6 w - - 0 58"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/4kp2/4r3/8/R7/8/1K6_w_-_-_0_58"]

{ You have Black, a pawn up, and play to win. Your pawn on f6 still has a long way to go. }
58. Kc2 { His king sets off toward your pawn. What now? } 58... Rd5 { That is the one: your rook on d5 shuts the whole d column, so his king on c2 cannot cross it. Watch. [%tail] [%cal Gd5d1] } (58... f5 { The pawn runs at once, before the door is shut. } 59. Ra6+ Kd5 60. Kd3 f4 61. Ra5+ { A draw: his king came to d3, close to your pawn's road, and his rook checks your king from the side. [%csl Rd3] }) (58... Rc5+ { A check along the c column? } 59. Kd3 Rd5+ 60. Ke4 { A draw: your checks only drove his king to e4, right beside your pawn's road. [%csl Re4] }) (58... Re2+ { A check from e2 instead. } 59. Kd3 Rh2 60. Ra6+ Ke5 61. Ra5+ { A draw: you checked instead of shutting the d column, his king stepped onto it at d3, and his rook keeps checking your king from the side. [%csl Rd3] }) (58... Kf5 { Your king steps forward to lead your pawn. } 59. Ra1 Kf4 60. Rf1+ { A draw: your rook on e5 shuts only one column, so his king still has the d column, and his rook checks your king from f1. [%cal Rf1f4] }) (58... Kd5 { Your king steps toward his. } 59. Kd3 Ke6 60. Ra6+ { A draw: his king reached d3 and his rook checks from the side; with only the e column shut, his king is close enough to help. [%csl Rd3] }) (58... Kd6 { Your king steps sideways, to d6. } 59. Ra6+ Kd5 60. Kd3 { A draw: his king is on d3 already, facing yours, and your rook on e5 shuts only the e column. [%csl Rd3] }) (58... Ke7 { Your king steps backward, to e7. } 59. Ra7+ Ke6 60. Ra6+ { A draw: his rook checks your king back and forth, and with only the e column shut, the d column stays open for his king. [%cal Rc2d3] }) 59. Ra1 Ke5 { Your king leads your pawn from e5, with the d and e columns between his king and your pawn. Alekhine, then world champion, chose this cut-off: it wins, though slowly. [%cal Gd5d1] } 0-1


[Event "Rook endgames: the bridge, out of the corner"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/tnoTgb8r"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Source "Lichess puzzle GZMLV, rated 1608, https://lichess.org/4hvtYQVB#159"]
[FEN "6R1/3K4/3P4/8/8/3r4/7p/7k w - - 8 80"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/6R1/3K4/3P4/8/8/3r4/7p/7k_w_-_-_8_80"]

{ You have Black. The bridge, or the Lucena after a Spanish writer of five hundred years ago: your king walks out from in front of your pawn, and your rook steps in between, so his rook can no longer stop your pawn. }
80. Rd8 { His rook goes to d8, and that was a mistake: his own king and pawn block the d column, so it is a move too slow to check you from the side. Walk out now: king to g2. [%cal Yh1g2] } 80... Kg2 { The first half of the bridge: your king leaves the corner, and the h1 square is free for your pawn. [%cal Gh2h1] } (80... Kg1 { Out of the corner the other way, to g1? } 81. Rg8+ Kh1 82. Rg4 { A draw: his check drove your king back into the corner, and his rook on g4 now holds the g column. [%cal Rg4g1] }) (80... Rxd6+ { Grab his pawn with check? } 81. Kxd6 Kg1 82. Rg8+ Kh1 83. Ra8 { A draw: you swapped your rook for one pawn, and your lone pawn cannot beat his rook. [%csl Rh2] }) (80... Rg3 { Your rook steps onto the g column first. } 81. Ra8 Kg2 82. Ra2+ Kg1 83. Ra1+ { A draw: your king came out, but his rook got round to the side, and now it checks along the rows with no shelter for your king. [%cal Ra1g1] }) 81. Rg8+ { He checks along the g column, from g8. Now the second half: your rook steps in between, to g3. [%cal Yd3g3,Rg8g2] } 81... Rg3 { Your rook stands between his rook and your king, and your king on g2 guards it: the check is blocked. That is the bridge at work. [%cal Rg8g2][%csl Gg3] } (81... Kf1 { Your king walks on, away from the check. } 82. Rh8 Rxd6+ 83. Kxd6 { A draw: with no rook in between, his rook got to h8, and your rook gave itself for his pawn; rook against your lone pawn is only a draw. [%cal Rh8h2] }) (81... Kf2 { Your king steps aside to f2. } 82. Rh8 Rxd6+ 83. Kxd6 { A draw: his rook came round to h8 against your pawn, your rook went for his pawn, and one pawn cannot beat his rook. [%cal Rh8h2] }) (81... Kf3 { Your king walks to f3. } 82. Rh8 Rxd6+ 83. Kxd6 { A draw: your king walked away from your pawn, his rook hit it from h8, and after your rook took his pawn, his rook holds your pawn. [%cal Rh8h2] }) (81... Kh1 { Back into the corner, out of the check. } 82. Rg4 { A draw: your king is back on h1, and his rook on g4 shuts the g column. [%cal Rg4g1] }) (81... Kh3 { Your king hides on h3, next to your pawn. } 82. Rh8+ Kg2 83. Rxh2+ Kxh2 84. Kc6 { A draw: his rook gave itself for your pawn, and your rook alone cannot beat his pawn with his king beside it. [%csl Rd6] }) 82. Rh8 { His rook swings to h8 and attacks your pawn along the h column. Step in between: rook to h3. [%cal Yg3h3,Rh8h2] } 82... Rh3 { Your rook blocks the h column, guarded by your king on g2: his rook can no longer stop your pawn. [%cal Rh8h2][%csl Gh3] } (82... h1=Q { Queen at once? } 83. Rxh1 Kxh1 84. Kc6 Rg8 85. d7 { A draw: his rook ate the new queen at once, you took it back, and his pawn on d7, with his king beside it, holds off your rook. [%csl Rd7] }) (82... Rg7+ { A check from g7 first? } 83. Kc6 h1=Q 84. Rxh1 Kxh1 85. Kb6 { A draw: the check only moved his king, his rook still ate the new queen, and your rook alone cannot win against his pawn. [%csl Rd6] }) (82... Kh1 { Your king steps back to guard the pawn from h1. } 83. Rh4 { A draw: your king stands in front of your pawn, so the pawn cannot move, and his rook keeps it under fire. [%cal Rh4h2] }) (82... Rg4 { Your rook steps aside, to g4. } 83. Kc6 h1=Q 84. Rxh1 Kxh1 85. d7 { A draw: nothing stood in between on the h column, so his rook took the new queen, and his pawn on d7 now holds off your rook. [%csl Rd7] }) (82... Kg1 { Your king steps to g1. } 83. Kc6 h1=Q+ 84. Rxh1+ Kxh1 85. d7 { A draw: his rook was still aimed along the h column, so the new queen fell at once, and his pawn on d7 saves him. [%csl Rd7] }) 83. Rxh3 { He trades the rooks, but too late for any pawn race: his king still blocks his own pawn, and yours stands one step from h1. Take back. [%cal Yg2h3] } 83... Kxh3 { Your king takes back on h3, right beside your pawn, and nothing can stop it now. Watch. [%tail] } (83... h1=Q { A queen right away, instead of taking back on h3? } 84. Rxh1 Kxh1 85. Ke6 Kg2 86. d7 { You lose: his rook ate your new queen, you took back, and now his d pawn stands one step from queening with your king far away. [%csl Rd8] }) 84. Kc7 h1=Q { A new queen on h1! Your king walked out of the corner and your rook stepped in between, twice: that was the bridge. [%csl Gh1] } 0-1


[Event "Rook endgames: the bridge, the king in front of its pawn"]
[Result "1-0"]
