{ You have White and play to win. Your pawn on e7 is one step from queening, but your own king sits in its way. }
59... Rd3 { His rook swings to d3, and that was a mistake. Your king is boxed in on e8, in front of its own pawn. What now? [%cal Rd3d8] } 60. Rg6+ { Check! His king has to leave g8, and that frees f7 for your king. } (60. Rxh3 { Grab his pawn? } 60... Rxh3 { You lose: his rook was guarding h3 along the third row, so it simply takes yours. [%cal Rd3h3] }) (60. Rh8+ { A check from h8? } 60... Kxh8 { You lose: his king simply takes your rook. [%csl Rh8] }) (60. Rf6 { Cut his king off the f column? } 60... h2 61. Rf1 Rh3 { A draw: his pawn ran to h2, your rook is stuck on the first row to stop it, and his rook now guards the pawn from h3. [%cal Rh3h1] }) (60. Rh4 { Your rook drops back to stop his pawn. } 60... Kg7 { A draw: his king steps to g7, and your king on e8 has no square left to walk out to. [%csl Re8] }) 60... Kh7 { His king steps to h7 and attacks your rook. What now? } 61. Kf7 { Your king walks out to f7: it guards your rook and leaves e8 free for the pawn. } (61. Kf8 { Your king steps out to f8 instead. } 61... Rf3+ 62. Rf6 Rxf6+ { You lose: on f8 your king does not guard f6, so his rook takes yours with check. [%csl Rf6] }) (61. Rg7+ { A check from g7? } 61... Kh6 { His king steps aside: taking your rook would leave you stalemated. } 62. Kf7 Rf3+ { A draw: now his rook checks you from f3, along the f column, and nothing can step in between. [%cal Rf3f7] }) (61. Rh6+ { A check from h6? } 61... Kxh6 { You lose: his king takes your rook, and his rook and pawn remain. [%csl Rh6] }) (61. Rd6 { Your rook goes to d6 to fight his rook? } 61... Rxd6 { You lose: his rook takes yours first, and your pawn cannot queen by itself. [%csl Rd6] }) 61... Rf3+ { Check from f3, along the f column. What now? } 62. Rf6 { Your rook steps in between, guarded by your king: the check is blocked, and nothing stands in your pawn's way now. That is the bridge. [%tail] [%cal Rf3f7][%csl Gf6] } (62. Ke8 { Your king goes back to e8? } 62... Kxg6 { You lose: from e8 your king no longer guards the rook, and his king takes it. [%csl Rg6] }) (62. Ke6 { Your king walks on to e6? } 62... h2 63. e8=Q Re3+ { You lose: his rook checks your king on e6 and hits your new queen on e8 through it, a skewer. [%cal Re3e8] }) 62... Re3 63. e8=Q Rxe8 64. Kxe8 Kg7 65. Rf3 h2 66. Rh3 Kf6 67. Rxh2 { The bridge did it: your pawn queened, his rook had to take her, and his bare king faces your king and rook. His king to g7, instead of the rook to d3, would have held the draw. } 1-0


[Event "Rook endgames: the Philidor, his pawn reaches your row"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/uHk954GX"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]
[Source "Tartakower - Spielmann, Bled 1931 (pgnmentor)"]
[FEN "8/5k2/1r6/R4PK1/8/8/8/8 w - - 1 88"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/5k2/1r6/R4PK1/8/8/8/8_w_-_-_1_88"]

{ You have Black, a pawn down: hold the draw. This is the Philidor defence, after a French master of 250 years ago: your king in front of his pawn, your rook on the sixth row, so his king cannot climb. [%cal Gb6h6] }
88. f6 { He pushes his pawn to f6, onto the row of your rook. Now your rook goes to the far end, b1, to check his king from behind. [%cal Yb6b1] } 88... Rb1 { The Philidor's rook move! Now if his king steps beside the pawn, the pawn on f6 cannot shield it from your checks. [%cal Gb1g1] } (88... Rb2 { The rook goes to b2 instead: also far enough from his king to check it from behind. } 89. Ra7+ Kf8 { This holds too, like b1: your king stays in front of the pawn, and your rook waits far behind his king. [%csl Gb2,Gf8] }) (88... Rb3 { The rook goes to b3: still far enough behind his king. } 89. Ra7+ Kf8 { This holds as well: your king blocks the pawn's road, and your rook waits behind his king. [%csl Gb3,Gf8] }) (88... Rb4 { The rook goes only as far as b4. } 89. Ra7+ Kf8 { Even this holds: your king stays in front of the pawn and your rook is still behind his king. The far end is simply the clearest square. [%csl Gb4,Gf8] }) (88... Rb7 { The rook steps to b7 instead, guarding the seventh row. } 89. Kf5 { This holds too, another way: your rook on b7 guards the seventh row, so his rook has no check along it. [%cal Gb7a7] }) (88... Rd6 { Your rook stays on the sixth row. But now his pawn stands on that row too. } 89. Ra7+ Ke8 90. Kg6 { His king steps forward beside the pawn, and your rook on d6 has no safe check: the pawn on f6 blocks the row. You lose. [%cal Rd6f6] }) (88... Rc6 { Your rook stays on the sixth row, on c6. } 89. Ra7+ Ke8 90. Kg6 { His king steps forward beside the pawn, and the pawn on f6 blocks every safe check along the row. You lose. [%cal Rc6f6] }) (88... Re6 { Your rook stays on the sixth row, right next to his pawn. } 89. Ra7+ Ke8 90. Kg6 { His king steps forward beside the pawn, and the pawn on f6 stands between your rook and his king. You lose. [%cal Re6f6] }) (88... Ra6 { The rook slides to a6, still on the sixth row. } 89. Rxa6 { His rook simply takes yours, a whole rook. You lose. [%csl Ra6] }) (88... Rb5+ { Your rook checks along the fifth row, but right next to his rook. } 89. Rxb5 { His rook simply took yours, for nothing. You lose. [%csl Rb5] }) (88... Rxf6 { Your rook grabs his pawn, and your king guards it there, for now. } 89. Ra7+ Ke6 90. Ra6+ Kd5 91. Rxf6 { His check from a6 hit your king and the rook behind it. A whole rook down: you lose. [%cal Ra6f6] }) (88... Ke8 { Your king steps off f7, to e8. } 89. Kg6 { That freed g6: his king steps forward beside the pawn, and your rook, still on the sixth row, has no check from behind. You lose. [%csl Rg6] }) (88... Kf8 { Your king steps back to f8. } 89. Kg6 { His king jumps to g6, the square your king stopped guarding, and your rook, still on the sixth row, has no check from behind. You lose. [%csl Rg6] }) (88... Kg8 { Your king steps to g8. } 89. Kg6 { His king walks straight to g6, beside the pawn, and your rook, still on the sixth row, has no check from behind. You lose. [%csl Rg6] }) (88... Ke6 { Your king steps forward to e6, next to the pawn. } 89. Kg6 { His king climbs to g6 and guards the pawn, and your king on e6 stands beside it, not in front. You lose. [%csl Rg6] }) 89. Ra7+ { He checks from the side. Step back to f8, in front of his pawn. [%cal Yf7f8] } 89... Kf8 { Right: your king now blocks the square his pawn needs, just as the Philidor wants. [%csl Gf8] } (89... Ke8 { The king steps to e8 instead, still guarding the pawn's road. } 90. f7+ Kf8 { This holds too: his pawn checks from f7, and your king steps right back in front of it. [%csl Gf8] }) (89... Kg8 { The king can also go to g8: from there it guards both squares in front of his pawn. } 90. Ra8+ Kf7 { This holds too: after his check your king steps back in front of the pawn, onto f7. [%csl Gf7] }) (89... Ke6 { Your king steps forward to e6, beside the pawn. } 90. Re7+ Kd6 91. Kh6 { His check pushed your king away from the pawn, and his own king heads for g7. You lose. [%csl Rg7] }) (89... Rb7 { Your rook blocks the check on b7. } 90. Rxb7+ Ke6 91. f7 { His rook simply took yours, and the pawn runs to f7. You lose. [%csl Rf7] }) 90. Kg6 { His king climbs to g6, beside the pawn. Now the check from behind: rook to g1! [%cal Yb1g1] } 90... Rg1+ { Check from behind! The pawn on f6 gives his king no shelter from your rook. That is the Philidor at work. [%cal Gg1g6] [%tail] } (90... Ke8 { Your king steps aside to e8 instead of checking. } 91. f7+ Kf8 92. Ra8+ { His pawn reached f7 with check, and his rook drives your king off the back row. You lose. [%cal Ra8f8] }) (90... Kg8 { Your king slips across to g8. } 91. Ra8+ Rb8 92. Rxb8# { Mate on the back row: your king on g8 has no square left. [%csl Rg8] }) (90... Rf1 { The rook goes behind his pawn, on f1. } 91. Ra8# { Mate! From behind the pawn your rook gives no check, and his rook takes the back row. [%csl Rf8] }) (90... Rb6 { Your rook goes back to the sixth row. } 91. Ra8+ Rb8 92. Rxb8# { Mate on the back row: with his king on g6 your rook had no check, and your king no square. [%csl Rf8] }) 91. Kf5 Rb1 92. Kg6 Rg1+ 93. Kf5 Rb1 94. Kg6 Rg1+ { The same position for the third time: each time his king climbs to g6, your rook checks it from g1, behind. A draw, by the Philidor. [%cal Gg1g6] } 0-1


