{ You have Black, a pawn down: hold the draw with the Philidor. Your rook already holds the sixth row. [%cal Gc6e6] }
61. Rg5+ { His rook checks from g5 to push your king off its post. What now? } 61... Kf7 { Right: your king steps back toward his pawn's road, ready to block it, and your rook still guards the sixth row. Now watch his checks. [%cal Gc6h6] [%tail] } (61... Kf6 { The king steps to f6 instead: also close to his pawn's road. } 62. e5+ Ke7 { This holds too: his pawn checks, and your king steps back to e7, right in front of it. [%csl Ge7] }) (61... Kh6 { Your king attacks his rook on g5. Tempting! } 62. Rg1 Rf6+ 63. Ke5 Ra6 64. Kf5 { His rook on g1 cuts your king off on the h column, far from his pawn, and his king leads it from f5. You lose. [%cal Rg1g7] }) (61... Kh7 { Your king steps away to h7. } 62. Kf5 Kh6 63. e5 Rc1 64. Rg2 { His rook on g2 shuts your king out on the h column, and his king and pawn walk forward together. You lose. [%cal Rg2g7] }) 62. Rf5+ Ke6 63. Re5+ Kd6 64. Rd5+ Ke6 65. Re5+ Kd6 66. Rd5+ Ke6 67. Re5+ Kd6 { Third time, the same position: his rook checks, your king steps between d6 and e6, and your rook waits on the sixth row. A draw: the Philidor. } 0-1


[Event "Rook endgames: the long side, his king looks for shelter"]
