[FEN "4R3/r4K1k/5P2/8/8/8/8/8 w - - 3 75"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4R3/r4K1k/5P2/8/8/8/8/8_w_-_-_3_75"]

{ You have Black, a pawn down: hold the draw. The long side is the side of his pawn with more columns: from there your rook checks his king from the side, too far away to be chased. [%cal Ga7f7] }
75. Ke6 { Spielmann held this against Tartakower in 1928, two of the strongest players of their day. His king steps to e6: check it from a6, the long side! [%cal Ya7a6] } 75... Ra6+ { Check from the long side: his f pawn has five columns on that side, and your rook stands on the last of them, out of his king's reach. [%cal Ga6e6] } (75... Re7+ { Your rook checks from close by, on e7. } 76. Rxe7+ { His rook took yours on e7: the close check simply gave it away. You lose. [%csl Re7] }) (75... Ra1 { The rook goes to the far end, the Philidor's move, but your king is not in front of his pawn. } 76. f7 Ra6+ 77. Kd5 Ra5+ 78. Kc4 { His pawn reached f7, and his rook on e8 guards the queening square: your checks only delay the new queen. You lose. [%cal Re8f8] }) (75... Kg6 { Your king steps toward his pawn, to g6. } 76. Rg8+ Kh6 77. f7 { His check drove your king back, and the pawn reached f7 next to his king. You lose. [%csl Rf7] }) (75... Kh6 { Your king steps to h6. } 76. f7 Ra6+ 77. Kd5 { Your king move gave him time: the pawn stepped on to f7, and his rook on e8 guards its queening square. You lose. [%cal Re8f8] }) 76. Kf5 { His king steps to f5. Check it from a5, still on the long side! [%cal Ya6a5] } 76... Ra5+ { Check from the long side, five columns from his pawn: too far away for his king to chase. Now watch him try. [%cal Ga5f5] [%tail] } (76... Rxf6+ { Your rook takes the pawn with check. } 77. Kxf6 { His king takes back: nothing guarded your rook there. You lose. [%csl Rf6] }) (76... Ra1 { The rook goes to the far end, a1. } 77. f7 Rf1+ 78. Ke6 Kg7 79. Rg8+ { His pawn on f7 guards his rook's check from g8, and your king must give way. You lose. [%csl Rf7] }) (76... Rd6 { Your rook slides along the sixth row to d6, to attack his pawn. } 77. f7 Rd5+ 78. Ke4 Rd1 79. f8=Q { His new queen stands on f8. From d6 your rook gave no check, and his pawn, already on the sixth row, simply stepped on. You lose. [%csl Rf8] }) (76... Kh6 { Your king steps off to h6. } 77. Rh8# { Mate on the h column: his rook checks, and his king and pawn guard the rest. [%cal Rh8h6] }) 77. Ke6 Ra6+ 78. Kf5 Ra5+ 79. Ke6 Ra6+ { The same position for the third time: his king walks between e6 and f5, and your rook checks it from the long side every time. A draw. [%cal Ga6e6] } 0-1


[Event "Rook endgames: the long side, his king slips out of the check"]
