{ You have Black, a pawn down: hold the draw. This is a long side ending, and your rook has just given check. [%cal Gf2f6] }
77. Ke7 { His king slips out of your check to e7, next to his pawn. Where does your rook belong? } 77... Ra2 { Yes: the far end of the long side, four columns from his pawn. From there your rook can check his king from the side. [%cal Ga2a7] } (77... Rh2 { The rook goes to the short side, h2. } 78. Rf1 Rh7+ 79. Kd6 Rh6 80. Kd7 Rh7+ 81. e7 { From the short side your check runs across his pawn's path: the pawn steps to e7 and blocks it. You lose. [%cal Rh7d7] }) (77... Rg2 { The rook goes to g2, on the short side. } 78. Rf1 Kg7 79. Re1 Ra2 80. Rg1+ { Too late for the long side: his rook checks your king toward the h column, and his king guards the pawn. You lose. [%cal Rg1g7] }) (77... Rb2 { Your rook goes to the long side, but only to b2. } 78. Rf8+ Kg7 79. Ra8 Rb7+ 80. Kd6 Rb6+ 81. Kd7 { Your rook on the b column is too close: his king walks forward next to the pawn, and a check from b7 would only let it attack your rook. You lose. [%csl Rb6] }) (77... Rc2 { Your rook stops on c2, two columns from his pawn. } 78. Rf1 Rc7+ 79. Kd6 Rc8 80. Kd7 { Too close: his king answered your check by chasing your rook. Now his king on d7 stands next to the pawn and still attacks your rook. You lose. [%csl Rc8] }) (77... Rd2 { Your rook slides only to d2, one column from his king. } 78. Rf1 Rd7+ 79. exd7 { From d2 your only check came from d7, right next to his king, and his pawn took the rook. You lose. [%csl Rd7] }) (77... Re2 { The rook goes behind his pawn, on e2. } 78. Rf1 Kg7 79. Rg1+ Kh6 80. Kd7 { Behind the pawn your rook had no safe check while his king stood on e7. His rook drove your king to the h column, and his king stepped aside to d7. You lose. [%cal Rg1g7] }) (77... Rxf7+ { Your rook takes his rook with check. } 78. exf7+ Kg7 79. f8=Q+ { His pawn took back with check and has just become a queen. You lose. [%csl Rf8] }) (77... Kh8 { Your king steps into the corner. } 78. Rxf2 { His rook came along the f column and took yours, left alone there. You lose. [%csl Rf2] }) 78. Rf4 { His rook steps to f4, ready to check your king from g4. What now? } 78... Ra7+ { Check from the long side! Your rook on a7 is four columns from his king, too far away to be chased. Now watch. [%cal Ga7e7] [%tail] } (78... Kg7 { Your king steps to g7 instead of checking. } 79. Rg4+ Kh6 80. Kd6 { His check drove your king to the h column, his rook on g4 keeps it there, and his king walks on. You lose. [%cal Rg4g7] }) (78... Kh7 { Your king steps to h7. } 79. Kf6 Ra6 80. Kf7 { His king walked forward next to the pawn, to f7, and your rook arrived too late. You lose. [%csl Rf7] }) (78... Kh8 { Your king goes to the corner. } 79. Kf7 Rh2 80. e7 { His king stands on f7 and his pawn on e7, one step from queening. You lose. [%csl Re8] }) 79. Ke8 Ra8+ 80. Ke7 Ra7+ 81. Ke8 Ra8+ 82. Ke7 Ra7+ { The same position for the third time: his king steps between e7 and e8, and your rook checks it from the long side each time. A draw. [%cal Ga7e7] } 0-1


[Event "Rook endgames: the active rook, the guard that waits"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/dx1dEdwg"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Source "Mason - Lasker, Hastings 1895 (pgnmentor)"]
[FEN "5k2/5r2/8/6RK/7P/5p2/8/8 w - - 4 76"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/5k2/5r2/8/6RK/7P/5p2/8/8_w_-_-_4_76"]

{ Last idea: the active rook. An active rook attacks his pawns or checks his king, so he must answer it; a passive rook only guards and waits. You have Black: play to win. }
76. Rg1 { His rook goes to g1 and guards your queening square from the side, so your pawn cannot get through, and your rook behind it only waits. Make your rook active: check on h7. [%cal Rg1f1,Yf7h7] } 76... Rh7+ { Check on h7: now your rook is active. It attacks his king, so he must answer it at once. [%cal Gh7h5] } (76... Rf6 { Rook to f6 keeps guarding your pawn from behind and waits. That is passive: his rook already holds your queening square, so the pawn can never get through. } 77. Kg5 f2 78. Rf1 { His rook on f1 blocks your pawn, and his king attacks your rook. Your rook behind the pawn just waits, and that is only a draw. [%csl Rf1] }) (76... Rf4 { Rook to f4 stays behind your pawn and hits his h pawn, but his king guards it, and his rook still holds your queening square. } 77. Kg5 f2 78. Rf1 Rf3 { His king chased your rook to f3, and his rook on f1 blocks your pawn. Your rook can only wait there, so it is a draw. }) (76... Rf5+ { A check from f5, but your rook stays behind your pawn, and his king answers by stepping to g4, next to both. } 77. Kg4 f2 78. Rf1 Ra5 { Your rook had to run from his king, and his rook on f1 still blocks your pawn: only a draw. }) (76... Rg7 { Rook to g7 attacks his rook, but gives no check, so he has time to move it and keep holding your queening square. } 77. Re1 f2 78. Rf1 { His rook slid along the first row to f1, in front of your pawn: only a draw. Lasker, world champion for 27 years, played rook to g7 here, and the game was drawn. [%csl Rf1] }) (76... f2 { Pawn to f2 walks straight into his guard: his rook blocks it on f1, and your rook behind it can only wait. } 77. Rf1 Rf3 78. Kg4 Ra3 { His king chased your rook off the f column. His rook stands in front of your pawn: only a draw. }) (76... Ke7 { Your king walks to the middle, but your rook still only guards, and his rook is free to check. } 77. Re1+ Kd6 78. Rd1+ { His rook checks your king along the d column, and your pawn still cannot get through: only a draw. }) (76... Ke8 { King to e8 steps away from the fight, and your rook still only guards. } 77. Re1+ Kd7 78. Kg6 { His king walks up to your rook, and his rook still holds your queening square: this draws. [%csl Rg6] }) 77. Kg4 { His king steps to g4, onto the same column as his own rook. Check him again, on g7. [%cal Yh7g7,Rg4g1] } 77... Rg7+ { Check on the g column: his king and his rook stand on the same column, so when the king steps aside your rook takes the rook behind it. That is called a skewer. [%cal Gg7g1] } (77... Rxh4+ { Taking his h pawn with check looks good, but your rook lands next to his king. } 78. Kxh4 { His king takes your rook, and his rook is left against your lone pawn: you lose. [%csl Rh4] }) (77... f2 { Pawn to f2 lets his rook step in front of it, and your chance to win his rook is gone. } 78. Rf1 Rh6 79. Rxf2+ { His rook took your pawn with check. His rook and pawn against your rook is only a draw. }) 78. Kf4 { His king steps aside, to f4, and attacks your pawn. Nothing stands between your rook and his rook now: take it. [%cal Yg7g1] } 78... Rxg1 { Your active rook takes his rook, the prize of the check on g7: you are a whole rook up now. [%tail] [%csl Gg1] } 79. Kxf3 Re1 80. Kf2 Re8 81. Kg2 Kf7 82. Kf2 Kg6 83. Kf3 Kh5 84. Kf4 Kxh4 { Your king takes his last pawn: rook against bare king, a win. Two checks from your active rook won his rook. [%csl Gh4] } 0-1


