51. Kd5 { His king steps to d5 and attacks your rook: a mistake. Your rook needs a job, not just a safe square. What now? } 51... Re3 { Rook to e3: an active rook, attacking his g3 pawn along the third row. His own rook has no safe square to guard it from. [%cal Ge3g3] } (51... Re1 { Rook to e1 runs far away and only waits: a passive rook. } 52. Rc3 { His rook steps back to guard his pawn from the side, and your rook on e1 attacks nothing: only a draw. [%cal Rc3g3] [%csl Re1] }) (51... Rf6 { Rook to f6 just steps out of the attack: a passive rook, with nothing to hit. } 52. Ke5 Rf5+ 53. Ke6 Rf6+ { His king chases your rook up and down the f column, and his g3 pawn is still safe: only a draw. }) (51... Re4 { Rook to e4 steps right next to his king, where nothing of yours guards it. } 52. Rxe4 { His rook takes yours: a rook against your one pawn, and you lose. }) (51... Rd6+ { A check from d6, but right next to his king. } 52. Kxd6 { His king simply takes your rook, and his rook is far too strong for your pawn: you lose. }) (51... Re5+ { A check from e5, but his king can take it. } 52. Kxe5 { His king takes the rook, and his own rook beats your pawn: you lose. }) (51... Kf5 { King to f5 guards your rook, but his rook checks your king away. } 52. Rf4+ Kg5 53. Kxe6 { His king took your rook, left alone on e6: you lose. [%csl Re6] }) 52. Rc8 { His rook runs to c8, ready to check your king. } 52... Rxg3 { His last pawn is gone, taken by your active rook: now it is your rook and pawn against his rook. His king on d4, guarding e3, would have held the draw. [%cal Bd4e3] [%csl Gg3] } (52... Rd3+ { A check first gives his king time to come closer. } 53. Ke4 Rxg3 54. Rg8+ { You took his pawn a move late: his king on e4 is close to your pawn now, and it is only a draw. }) 0-1


