{ You have White. A pawn each: hold the draw. }
67... g4 { His g pawn steps to g4, next to his king, and your rook stands guard over your h pawn. Hold the draw: what now? } 68. Rf6+ { Check on f6: an active rook. His king must answer it, and your rook now guards the sixth row, where your h pawn can step to safety. [%cal Gf6h6] } (68. Rh7 { Rook to h7 keeps guarding your h pawn and waits: a passive rook. His king walks forward with his pawn. } 68... Kg3 69. Ke3 Kh3 70. Rg7 Rxh5 { His king came round to h3, and his rook took your h pawn: his rook and pawn against your rook, and you lose. }) (68. Rh8 { Rook to h8 only waits at the far end, and his king is free to walk. } 68... Kg3 69. Ke3 Kh3 { His king stands on h3 next to his pawn, while your rook on h8 only guards: you lose. }) (68. Rg6 { Rook to g6 gets behind his g pawn, but lets go of your own h pawn. } 68... Rxh5 { His rook takes your h pawn, and his king on f4 guards his own pawn: you lose. }) (68. Ke2 { King to e2 walks toward his pawn, but his king gets to it first. } 68... Kg3 69. Ke3 Kh3 { His king stands on h3, next to his pawn, and yours is a step too late: you lose. }) (68. Kd3 { King to d3 heads for his pawn, but his king steps forward first. } 68... Kf3 69. Rf6+ Kg2 { His king walked on to g2 after the check, in front of his pawn, and your king is too far away: you lose. }) 68... Kg3 { His king steps forward to g3, and your h pawn on h5 hangs to his rook. } 69. h6 { Pawn to h6: now your rook on f6 guards it along the sixth row, so his rook cannot take it for free. [%cal Gf6h6] } (69. Rf5 { Rook to f5 also holds: it guards your h pawn along the fifth row. [%cal Gf5h5] } 69... Kh2 { His king goes to h2, and your rook still holds the draw from the f column, with a check or by hitting his g pawn. }) 69... Kh2 { His king walks on to h2, ready to bring his pawn forward. } 70. Rf4 { Rook to f4 attacks his g pawn, so his rook must stay on the fourth row to guard it. That is your active rook at work. [%cal Gf4g4] } (70. h7 { Pawn to h7 runs for a queen, but his rook on h4 stands behind it. } 70... g3 71. h8=Q Rxh8 { His rook took your new queen from behind, and his g pawn with his king next to it is too strong: you lose. }) (70. Rf2+ { A check from f2 sends his king to g1, where your rook can no longer check it safely. } 70... Kg1 { From g1 his king attacks your rook on f2: you lose. }) 70... Kg3 { His king steps to g3 and attacks your rook on f4. } 71. Rf6 { Rook to f6 again: it guards your h pawn from the side, ready to hit his g pawn from f4 the moment his king leaves. [%cal Gf6h6] [%tail] } (71. Rxg4+ { Taking his g pawn with check looks tempting, but his king guards it. } 71... Kxg4 { His king takes your rook: his rook against your h pawn, and you lose. }) 71... Kh2 72. Rf4 Kg3 73. Rf6 Kh2 74. Rf4 { The same position for the third time: a draw, held by your active rook. Wolf, an Austrian master, played king to e2 at the start instead of the check and should have lost; Tartakower, one of the world's best, missed the win. } 1-0
